            "Enter a text: tell about cricket\n",
            "**Cricket** is one of the world’s most popular bat-and-ball sports, played between two teams of 11 players each. It is often described as a game of strategy, skill, and patience, with a rich history and a massive global following, particularly in the British Commonwealth nations like India, England, Australia, Pakistan, and South Africa.\n",
            "\n",
            "Here is a comprehensive breakdown of the game:\n",
            "\n",
            "---\n",
            "\n",
            "### 1. The Object of the Game\n",
            "The primary goal is simple: **score more runs than the opposing team.** \n",
            "* The team that bats first tries to score as many runs as possible.\n",
            "* The team that fields and bowls tries to restrict the scoring and get the batters \"out.\"\n",
            "* Once the first team’s innings (turn to bat) is over, the roles switch.\n",
            "\n",
            "---\n",
            "\n",
            "### 2. The Playing Field and Equipment\n",
            "* **The Pitch:** A rectangular 22-yard (20.12 meters) long strip in the center of the field. Most of the action happens here.\n",
            "* **The Wickets:** At each end of the pitch are three wooden vertical stumps topped by two horizontal pieces of wood called bails. Bowlers try to hit these to get a batter out.\n",
            "* **The Bat:** Made of willow wood, typically English willow, with a cane handle.\n",
            "* **The Ball:** A hard, solid sphere usually made of cork covered with leather. (Red balls are used in multi-day matches, white balls in limited-overs matches).\n",
            "* **Protective Gear:** Because the ball is hard and travels fast, batters wear helmets, pads (leg guards), gloves, thigh guards, and chests guards.\n",
            "\n",
            "---\n",
            "\n",
            "### 3. How Runs are Scored\n",
            "Two batters are on the pitch at all times (one at each end). \n",
            "* **Running:** When the batter hits the ball with the bat, they can run to the opposite end of the pitch. Each successful one-way trip equals **1 run**.\n",
            "* **Boundaries:** \n",
            "  * If the ball is hit along the ground and crosses the boundary rope, it is worth **4 runs**.\n",
            "  * If the ball is hit through the air and lands outside the boundary rope, it is worth **6 runs** (a \"six\").\n",
            "\n",
            "---\n",
            "\n",
            "### 4. How Batters Get Out\n",
            "A bowler tries to dismiss the batter. There are 10 ways to get out, but the most common are:\n",
            "1. **Bowled:** The bowler hits the stumps directly with the ball, dislodging the bails.\n",
            "2. **Caught:** A fielder catches the ball in the air after the batter hits it with the bat (or gloves).\n",
            "3. **LBW (Leg Before Wicket):** The ball hits the batter's body (usually the leg) instead of the bat, and the umpire judges that the ball would have hit the stumps.\n",
            "4. **Run Out:** A fielder throws the ball and hits the stumps while the batters are still running between the wickets.\n",
            "5. **Stumped:** The wicketkeeper hits the stumps with the ball while the batter has stepped out of their crease to play a shot and missed.\n",
            "\n",
            "---\n",
            "\n",
            "### 5. The Three Main Formats of Cricket\n",
            "Cricket is played in different formats, ranging from fast-paced entertainment to grueling multi-day tests of skill:\n",
            "\n",
            "* **Test Cricket (The Traditional Format):**\n",
            "  * Played over **5 days**.\n",
            "  * Each team gets two innings.\n",
            "  * Considered the ultimate test of a player’s skill and mental endurance. Players wear traditional all-white clothing.\n",
            "\n",
            "* **One Day Internationals (ODIs):**\n",
            "  * Played over **one day**.\n",
            "  * Each team faces a fixed number of overs, usually **50 overs** (300 balls per innings).\n",
            "  * Famous for the quadrennial **ICC Cricket World Cup**.\n",
            "\n",
            "* **Twenty20 (T20):**\n",
            "  * The fastest and most explosive format.\n",
            "  * Each team faces just **20 overs** (120 balls). A match lasts about 3 hours.\n",
            "  * Highly commercialized, featuring major domestic leagues like the **Indian Premier League (IPL)** and the **Big Bash League (BBL)**, along with the T20 World Cup.\n",
            "\n",
            "---\n",
            "\n",
            "### 6. Key Terms to Know\n",
            "* **Overs:** A set of six consecutive legal deliveries (pitches) bowled by one bowler.\n",
            "* **Innings:** One team’s turn to bat (or bowl).\n",
            "* **Duck:** A score of zero runs by a batter.\n",
            "* **Century:** When a batter scores 100 or more runs in a single innings.\n",
            "* **Hat-trick:** When a bowler takes three wickets on three consecutive deliveries.\n",
            "\n",
            "---\n",
            "\n",
            "### Cultural Impact\n",
            "Cricket is more than just a sport in countries like India, Pakistan, and Bangladesh; it is practically a religion. In England and Australia, it is a cherished summer tradition often associated with leisurely afternoons, tea breaks, and intense rivalry (such as **The Ashes** test series between England and Australia). \n",
            "\n",
            "Today, cricket is expanding globally, driven largely by the fast-paced T20 format and its inclusion in major multi-sport events like the upcoming 2028 Summer Olympics in Los Angeles.\n"
          ]
